Materials and methods
Plant materials
To broadly cover terrestrial plants, we collected plant samples from various sources (
Supplementary Table S1
). A total of 153 plant species have been collected so far, with each species comprising one or more tissues, like leaf, flower, seed, stem or root. Three biological replicated samples were obtained for each tissue. The collected samples were immediately frozen in liquid nitrogen before stored at −80°C. For study cases 1 and 2, rice and maize samples were harvested in Jiangsu (Yangzhou, southeastern China) and in Hainan (Sanya, southern China), respectively.
DNA extraction, PCR amplification, ITS sequencing and annotation
Fresh leaf tissue was first ground into powder with liquid nitrogen. Total genomic DNA was subsequently extracted using the Plant Genomic DNA Kit (Tiangen Biotech Co., China) according to the manufacturer's instructions. The plant internal transcribed spacer 2 (ITS2) region was amplified using the following primers: ITS2-2F (5′-ATGCGATACTTGGTGTGAAT-3′) and ITS2-3R (5′-GACGCTTCTCCAGACTACAAT-3′). PCR was performed in 50 μl reaction mixtures, containing 5 μl of 10× Buffer (including 2.5 mM Mg
2+
), 1 μl of Taq polymerase (TaKaRa), 1.5 μl of each PCR primer (10 μM), 1 μl of dNTP (10mM) and 1 μl of DNA extract (20ng/μl), and the total volume was adjusted to 50 μl with sterile deionized water. PCR amplification was conducted according to the following procedure: 95°C for 5 min, 35 cycles of 95°C for 30 s, 58°C for 30 s and 72°C for 1.5 min, and a final extension at 72°C for 7 min. PCR products were analyzed by electrophoresis in a 1% agarose gel. The PCR products were purified using an AxyPrep DNA Gel Extraction Kit (AXYGEN, Hangzhou, China) and sequenced bidirectionally using a ABI 3730XL sequencer (Applied Biosystems Co., USA) based on the Sanger sequencing method at Shanghai Personalbio Technology Co., Ltd. The sequenced ITS2 sequences were aligned against the NCBI NT database with the megablast tool (
23
), and the species with the highest sequence similarity was selected.
Metabolite extraction and mass spectral analysis
The samples were processed for mass spectral analysis as described in our previous study (
24
). In brief, the freeze-dried tissues were ground using a tissue grinder (No. 05010997; Shanghai BiHeng Biotechnology Company Limited, Shanghai, China) for 2 min at 55 Hz. 150 mg of powder was weighed and mixed with 1.5 ml 70% aqueous methanol (containing 1 mg/l capsaicin as the internal standard). The samples were vortexed every 10 min three times and extracted overnight at 4°C. Following centrifugation at 12 000 g for 15 min, all the supernatants were filtered using a 0.22 μm filter and transferred to a liquid phase bottle for subsequent analysis.
Samples were analyzed on a Waters Acquity Ultra Performance Liquid Chromatography system equipped with an ACQUITY UPLC BEH C18 column (1.7 μm, 2.1 mm × 100 mm) coupled with Q Exactive hybrid Q-Orbitrap-high resolution mass spectrometry (Q-Orbitrap-HRMS) (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The instrument parameter settings used for the analysis followed the procedure outlined in our previous study (
24
).
Workflow for assembling the reference metabolome
The workflow for constructing the plant reference metabolome consisted of four major steps (Figure
1B
): (i) Raw MS data processing: Raw MS files were processed in Compound Discoverer software (version 3.2, Thermo Scientific) with automatic workflow for LC spectra alignment by retention time (RT) and peak detection. To refine the metabolite features, a set of criteria were applied (
25
), including reproducibility <90%, sample to blank ratio <10%, peak area less than 1E5, and elimination of features exhibiting multi-ion adducts (such as Na
+
, K
+
, NH
4
+
, Cl
−
), isotopes, in-source fragmentation, or dimerization. (ii) Assembly of reference metabolome: an in-house script based on Xcalibur Development Kit (XDK) in Xcalibur software was first used to automatically acquire MS1/MS2 paired data from the raw LC−MS/MS files derived from distinct tissues of the plant species. The repetitive features identified from these diverse tissues were merged to establish the reference metabolome, encompassing spectral features separately in both ESI positive and negative ion mode. The merging process adhered to the following parameters: mass tolerance 5 ppm; retention time tolerance 0.2 min. (iii) Annotation of reference metabolome: the annotation of the reference metabolome was carried out following our prior research (
24
). In summary, we employed two complementary approaches for this process. Initially, we searched against the experimental reference mass spectra library obtained from public databases and structurally annotated MS2 spectra. Subsequently, we utilized predicted theoretical spectra to further enhance the scope of the structural annotations. To achieve a hierarchical chemical classification of the compounds, we utilized the Ruby application programming interface within the ClassyFire software (
26
) to generate the chemical classes. (iv) Creating reference metabolome files: sample, instrument, MS data and annotation information were integrated to generate reference metabolome file in .msr or .mgf format.
Figure 1.
Integration of plant species and metabolome data. (
A
) A taxonomic tree depicting the taxonomic relationships among 153 plant species that are representative of the five major phyla: Bryophyta (red), Lycopodiopsida (purple), Pteridophyte (yellow), Gymnospermae (green) and Angiospermae. The color scheme for subgroups of Angiospermae: ANA grade, grey; Magnoliids, orange; Monocotyledons, magenta; and Dicotyledons, blue. (
B
) Workflow for constructing plant reference metabolome, which consists of four major steps: (i) raw MS data processing; (ii) assembly of reference metabolome; (iii) annotation of reference metabolome and (iv) creating reference metabolome files (detailed description in Materials and methods). (
C
) Global metabolic profiles of the reference metabolome for 153 plants. The innermost circuit depicts the taxonomic relationships of the 153 species. Circuits A–H display the profiles of metabolites belonging to eight chemical classes (A = alkaloids, B = amino acid derivatives, C = flavonoids, D = lipids, E = nucleotides and derivatives, F = phenols, G = phenylpropanoids, H = terpenoids). The intensity of color shading in circuits A-H indicates the number of metabolites detected for each species. The bar chart in circuit I represents the total number of detected metabolites. (
D
) The reference metabolome for
Callistemon rigidus
. The header section of .msr file is illustrated to the left. Metabolites are represented as dots, with chemical classifications indicated by their color. Examples of annotated and unknown metabolites are presented at the top and bottom, respectively.
Construction of standard compounds mass spectral libraries
The standard compounds mass spectral libraries consist of experimental MS/MS spectra and
in silico
spectra. The experimental MS/MS spectra data were sourced from a range of databases including MassBank (
8
), MoNA (
https://mona.fiehnlab.ucdavis.edu/
), ReSpect (
27
), FiehnLib (
28
) and RIKEN PlaSMA (
http://plasma.riken.jp/
). According to the use of specific keywords and meticulous filter criteria, the mass spectral data corresponding to the metabolites of interest under investigation were retrieved. For each individual metabolite, we procured its corresponding mass spectrum, molecular formula, molecular weight and potential metabolite information from these databases.
The
in silico
mass spectral data were generated by converting the structural information of biologically relevant compounds into theoretical spectrograms using CFM-ID software (
29
). The structural data for each compound were collected from four biologically relevant structure databases: KEGG (
30
), KNApSAcK (
31
), PubChem (
32
) and UNPD (
33
). For the KEGG database, ‘2Dmol’ files obtained via the ‘python-Bio.KEGG’ API were utilized to fill in missing structural information through the application of OpenBabel software (
34
), as SMILES, InChIKey and InChI were not available. In the case of the PubChem database, compounds from the PubChem classification browser, annotated with NCBI BioSystems (
35
), biological role classification from ChEBI Ontology (
36
), and classifications as flavonoids or prenol lipids from LIPID MAPS structure database (
37
) were downloaded.
Implementation of structure query and spectral query
The structure query was implemented via the function ‘Search metabolites’ using the ‘Structure Query’ tool. The JavaScript based molecule editor JSME (
38
) was employed to transform the drawn molecule structure into a canonical SMILES format. Subsequently, the SMILES representation of the query molecule was converted into an InChI string using the chemistry development kit (
39
). This InChI string was then utilized for a query against the plant reference metabolome to identify matching hits.
For the spectra query, the function ‘Search metabolites’, particularly in the ‘LC–MS/MS Query’, as well as related functions such as RefMetaBlast and CompoundLibBlast analytical tools, the INCOS algorithm (
40
) was employed through java scripts to score the similarity between reference mass spectra. In brief, the reference mass spectra that have similar precursor m/z (within a user-defined precursor mass tolerance range) with query mass spectra were retrieved and compared for similarity using the INCOS algorithm. Reference mass spectra with a similarity score >0.75 were used to assign annotation to query mass spectra.
Implementation of analytical tools ‘RefMetaBlast’ and ‘CompoundLibBlast’
The RefMetaBlast and CompoundLibBlast analytical tools, which can be used via the function ‘Analyze Spectra’, are designed for LC–MS/MS data annotation. These tools are intended for use with high-resolution LC–MS/MS data acquired in centroid mode and provided in standard formats such as mzML, mzXML or mzData. Upon submission of an annotation task, the LC–MS data undergoes initial processing using a custom R script built upon the XCMS package (
41
). This processing encompasses peak detection, peak filtering, and extraction of MS2 spectra. Subsequently, each MS2 spectrum is annotated by a database search via the INCOS algorithm. The RefMetaBlast tool can be used to analyze a sample's spectra with a reference metabolome from a specific species. The CompoundLibBlast tool analyzes a sample's spectra with public mass spectral data and
in silico
mass spectral data. In addition, the result of matched or annotated metabolites can be conveniently downloaded in both JSON and CSV formats.
Database and web interface implementation
The RefMetaPlant project was developed and integrated on the Java SSM framework (Spring, Spring MVC and MyBatis) and the packaged application was deployed on an Apache Tomcat web server (
http://tomcat.apache.org/
) which runs on the CentOS 6.8 system. The interactive RefMetaPlant web interface was developed using Bootstrap (3.3.7,
http://getbootstrap.com/
) and jQuery (2.1.0,
http://jquery.com/
) and implemented in JSP. All the data in RefMetaPlant were stored in a relational database MySQL (5.1.73,
http://www.mysql.com/
). The 2D and 3D structure of compounds were displayed using Smiles Drawer and JSmol (
http://wiki.jmol.org/index.php/JSmol
), respectively. The JavaScript plotting library Flot (
http://www.flotcharts.org/
) was used to draw interactive mass spectrum. The interactive result tables for annotated metabolites were generated using DataTables (
https://www.datatables.net/
). The back-end logic processing and data analysis were mainly implemented through R and Java scripts. The database is available online without registration and is optimized for Chrome, Opera, Firefox, Windows Edge and macOS Safari.
Statistical analysis
Statistical analyses were mainly conducted using R software (
https://www.R-project.org/
). Affinity Propagation (AP) clustering was employed to group metabolites with similar abundance profiles utilizing the ‘apcluster’ package in R (
42
). The taxonomic relationships among species were generated from the NCBI Taxonomy database (
43
), and visualized using the interactive Tree of Life (iTOL) web server (
44
). Other data analyses were carried out by in-house scripts written in R or Perl. The manuscript figures were mostly drawn by R.

Plant materials
To broadly cover terrestrial plants, we collected plant samples from various sources (
Supplementary Table S1
). A total of 153 plant species have been collected so far, with each species comprising one or more tissues, like leaf, flower, seed, stem or root. Three biological replicated samples were obtained for each tissue. The collected samples were immediately frozen in liquid nitrogen before stored at −80°C. For study cases 1 and 2, rice and maize samples were harvested in Jiangsu (Yangzhou, southeastern China) and in Hainan (Sanya, southern China), respectively.

