Introduction
Metabolomics is defined as the science of qualitative and quantitative measurement of small molecule metabolites in living systems. The metabolome of a cell, a tissue, or an organism refers to the collection of all small molecule metabolites present in that biological system (
1
,
2
). Plants are unique in that they have developed their complex secondary metabolism through more than 900 million years of evolution. The astounding chemical diversity and metabolic complexity in plants are highlighted by estimates suggesting that metabolites in green plants number in the millions (
3
). These metabolites play crucial roles in various aspects of plant biology, including growth, development, and responses to environmental challenges (
3
,
4
). Plant metabolites contribute to the intricate web of biochemical reactions and signaling pathways that orchestrate plant physiology. In this context, the emerging field of plant metabolomics has garnered substantial attention, offering new opportunities to uncover the intricate metabolic networks in plants.
In recent decades, the study of plant metabolism has undergone significant advancements, largely driven by the emergence of metabolomics. Due to the enormous diversity and structural complexity of small molecule metabolites across plant species, their identification and functional analysis pose a significant challenge to researchers (
5
). The advance of metabolomics analytical technology, for example high-resolution mass spectrometry (HRMS) coupled with ultra-performance liquid chromatography (UPLC) separation, makes genome-scale metabolomic analysis possible, enabling detection of metabolites at an unparalleled sensitivity, resolution, accuracy, and throughput (
3
,
4
,
6
,
7
). Metabolomic technology based on high-performance of liquid chromatography combining high-resolution mass spectrometry is suitable for detecting the broad range of liquid-soluble metabolites found in plants, thus has become an standard approach for plant metabolomics research.
Many databases and supporting tools related to metabolomics research and data sharing have been launched in the last two decades. Examples include MassBank (
8
), MassBase (
9
), HMDB (
10
), MetaboLights (
11
), Metabolomics Workbench (
12
), XMRs (
13
), RIKEN MetaDatabase (
14
), Metlin(
15
) and BinBase (
16
). While these resources provide many general tools for metabolomics data sharing and analysis, annotation with compound standard mass spectra, and profiling of MS-based metabolic data, they are not completely satisfactory when facing the many of the specific challenges of plant metabolomics research. One factor is that the rapid advance of MS technologies made many of the spectral repository data obsolete due to lower mass spectral qualities, i.e. accuracy, resolution, and sensitivity. Particularly, the shortcomings of the instruments and experimental conditions used to produce much of the existing MS data has resulted in limited coverage of metabolite peaks, reduced mass spectral quality, and lower analytical power. Furthermore, for plant metabolomics research, the lack of both reference metabolome and high-quality spectral data for standard compounds, as well as high-throughput analytical tools, has become a major bottleneck for the discovery and functional study of phytochemicals in plants.
To meet the needs of front-running phytochemical research, we have undertaken to address several challenges faced by plant metabolomics research: (i) We have generated untargeted mass spectral data, leveraging the most advanced LC–MS platform (UPLC-Q-Orbitrap-MS), from >150 plant species collected across five major phyla: Bryophyta, Lycopodiopsida, Pteridophyta, Gymnospermae and Angiospermae. (ii) We built a ‘Reference Metabolome Database for Plants’ (abbreviated as RefMetaPlant), using a computation protocol we developed for constructing a reference metabolome. Inspired by the reference genome concept, the reference plant metabolome was constructed by collecting and ‘assembling’ its metabolites’ mass spectral data (MS1/MS2) from all available organs/tissues of the species. The first release of RefMetaPlant contains the reference metabolome for >150 plants, and has functions for metabolic research that were aimed to parallel that of RefSeq (
17
) for genomic studies. (iii) We have integrated standard compound mass spectral libraries for annotation, including both experimental reference mass spectra and
in silico
mass spectra, and developed a set of related query and analytical tools like ‘LC–MS/MS Query’, ‘RefMetaBlast’ and ‘CompoundLibBlast’ for plants metabolome searches and profiling, and metabolite identification.
It is worth noting that many existing metabolic databases predominantly focus on model organisms and a small number of plant species (
18–21
), which poses significant limitations that hinder the exploration of the metabolomics space in the broad field of plant research. Therefore, we have established RefMetaPlant as a broader more integrated platform to enable increased interactions between scientists from different parts of the world and ease the flow of information within the scientific community (
22
). RefMetaPlant serves as an integrated database and analysis platform dedicated to becoming the centralized resource for plant metabolomic research. It aims to standardize and integrate reference metabolome data, providing a comprehensive platform for researchers in plant metabolomics, genetics, and related fields. RefMetaPlant is freely available at
https://www.biosino.org/RefMetaDB/
, which offers a user-friendly web interface and a variety of easy-to-use tools for the functions mentioned above. This comprehensive platform caters to a broad audience, including scientists in plant metabolomics research, geneticists in plant breeding, and even consumers interested in understanding the chemical contents of plants. RefMetaPlant was established to accelerate advancements in plant metabolomics research, foster collaborations and promote a deeper understanding of plant metabolism.
