Results
Constructing a reference metabolome for plants
A reference metabolome refers to the global collection of all metabolites for a given species. An analog comparison between the reference metabolome and metabolites for a given species mirrors that between the genome and genes for the species. In this study, the reference metabolome for a plant species is constructed by collecting and ‘assembling’ its metabolites’ mass spectral data (MS1/MS2) from all available organs/tissues of the species. Nowadays the high-resolution mass spectrometry can detect and identify a massive number of heterogeneous phytochemicals that have highly diverse structures and chemical and physical properties, usually numbering in the tens of thousands. Thus, in order to construct a plant reference metabolome an efficient computing process must be implemented to handle and resolve the mass spectral data, to eliminate redundant features, and to annotate detected metabolites.
To broadly cover terrestrial plants, we have so far collected tissue samples from more than 150 plant species that are representative of the five major phyla: Bryophyta, Lycopodiopsida, Pteridophyte, Gymnospermae, and Angiospermae (Figure
1A
, and
Supplementary Table S1
). Their classifications were validated by ITS sequencing on each plant sample (
Supplementary Table S2
).
Mass spectral analysis was performed using ultra-performance liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (UPLC–MS/MS) platform on the tissues collected for each plant species. The raw spectral data were processed and filtered to obtain high-quality feature signals (Figure
1B
