The function ‘Search metabolites’ supports querying the reference metabolome using five different types of queries, i.e. structure sketch, molecular weight, combined parameters, LC–MS and LC–MS/MS (Figure
3B
). For the latter two, queries can be submitted with a list of precursor ion (MS1), or a parent ion (MS1) with its fragment ions (MS2), and with other options for specified plants species, tolerance of
m
/
z
, or library for query. As a result, a list of matched metabolites is returned, which allows users to view the details of each metabolite by following a link. Importantly, all plant species that contain the metabolite in their reference metabolome are shown, revealing the ‘popularity’ of the metabolite. In addition, a filter box is also provided for the above tools for users to select data from the list of returned results by typing in a parameter, such as name, molecular weight, SMILE, classification, etc.
To facilitate the use of RefMeta database for plant metabolic research, we developed two related analytical tools, RefMetaBlast and CompoundLibBlast (Figure
3C
). RefMetaBlast analyzes a sample's spectra by comparing it with a selected reference metabolome for species-specific metabolic profiling. The reference metabolome selected for comparison provides a high-confidence reference for profiling of interested samples by users. The function ‘CompoundLibBlast’ analyzes a sample's spectra with selected compound libraries for automated metabolite annotation and identification. Its function is performed based on the experimental reference mass spectral data from public databases, and the
in silico
mass spectral data we generated (
24
,
29
). These spectral analytical tools are implemented through an interactive webpage with some advanced features that were not available before in an open-source tool. Query data in mzXML, mzML or mzData format are uploaded first. Data analysis is initiated by clicking the ‘Start analysis’ button, and a hyperlink is provided for long running analysis, to allow users to retrieve the results. Both RefMetaBlast and CompoundLibBlast functions return results of matched or annotated metabolites in addition to downloadable files containing extracted MS2 spectra (MGF format), and annotation data (CSV and JSON format) (Figure
3C
).
Case study 1: metabolic trends during the rice grain filling period
Grain filling is the growth stage in rice when fertilized ovaries acquire carbohydrate and other nutrients to synthesize starch and convert themselves into grains (
45
,
46
). It determines the yield and quality of cereal crops (
47
). Accumulation of metabolites in filling grains involves the coordination of different metabolic and cellular processes, understanding the dynamic changes of these substances, and is of great significance in study of seed development and crop breeding (
48
).
We applied RefMetaBlast to analyze the metabolic profile of
Oryza sativa
var. Yangdao #6 during the process of grain filling. Rice grains from 10, 20 and 30 days after flowering (DAF), were harvested and processed for mass spectrum experiments (Figure
4A
). Raw mass spectral data were extracted with ProteoWizard (
49
) before they were assigned using the rice reference metabolome and the online tool RefMetaBlast. Consequently, 468, 469 and 511 metabolites were detected above the threshold for the 10, 20 and 30 DAF grain samples, respectively (
Supplementary Table S5
). The numbers of different metabolites identified in the rice grains varied between different developmental stages (Figure
4B
). Lipid compounds were found to be the most abundant, followed by terpenoids, and flavonoids. Their numbers were generally trended higher at later growth stages except for amino acid derivatives, alkaloids and flavonoids.
