For the grain metabolites assigned by RefMetaBlast, their relative abundance was grouped with affinity propagation (AP) clustering to investigate their changes in their patterns. The changes of metabolite abundance were grouped into nine distinct clusters (Figure
4C
,
D
). Notably, about 46% of amino acid derivatives belonged to cluster II, which showed a trend of gradual reduction between 10 DAF and 30 DAF. A substantial number of terpenoids first increased between 10 DAF and 20 DAF, then decreased between 20 DAF and 30 DAF (cluster III, cluster VI and cluster VII; Figure
4C
,
D
). Lipids appeared to exhibit temporal specificity, with different types of lipids present at different time points. We observed a significant proportion of lipids in Clusters II (∼25%), which was high at 10 DAF, III (∼17%), which was high at 20 DAF, and VIII (∼21%), which was high at 30 DAF. About 15% of the flavonoids were found to increase from 10 DAF to 30 DAF (clusters VIII and XI). Notably, rhoifolin and apigenin 4′-glucuronide, two derivatives of apigenin that belong to flavonoid family, were found to only be present at 30 DAF. As they share a chemical structure resembling that of anthocyanins, it remains to be determined if they play a role in the yellowing process in rice grains. The similar kinetics of different groups of metabolites during rice grain development may suggest common regulation mechanisms for the related pathways, which can help direct further study. The above findings demonstrate the utility of RefMetaBlast as a tool, enabling a deeper understanding of the changes and regulatory mechanisms of metabolites during the growth and development of rice.
Case study 2: discovery and expanded annotation of metabolites in maize samples
The CompoundLibBlast tool was designed to annotate plant metabolites using expanded compound mass spectral libraries, including both experimental and
in silico
mass spectral data (
24
,
29
). To demonstrate its performance, we conducted an analysis of non-targeted metabolomic data for corn samples of
Zea mays
var. B73 (Figure
4E
), using CompoundLibBlast. We identified a total of 746 non-redundant metabolites, which belonged to eight chemical classes (Figure
4F
,
Supplementary Table S6
). Among these, the largest class of metabolites was lipids, which contained 170 compounds, highlighting the richness of lipids in corn.
To evaluate the performance of CompoundLibBlast, we compared its results with those reported in recently published studies (Figure
4G
). The number of metabolites annotated by our tool increased significantly compared to those of the early studies (
50–54
). We further compared our results with that of Liang and colleagues’ (
54
), which reported the most metabolites among previous studies. Our tool, CompoundLibBlast, was able to identify additional metabolites in corn, particularly some nutrition-related substances, such as vitamin K1, riboflavin, and linoleic acid, which were not found in the 2021 study (
54
) (Figure
4H
). Thus, CompoundLibBlast is shown to capture a significantly broader range of metabolites, identifying many that were previously overlooked. However, further experimental validation is necessary to establish the identities of these metabolites and their functions in biological contexts. This case proved CompoundLibBlast to be a valuable tool to expand the study of diverse metabolites in crops, offering great potentials for discovery of novel metabolites and for a deeper understanding of metabolism in plants.
Case study 3: specialization of secondary metabolites in the evolution of legumes
The evolving functions of secondary metabolites in plants has been subjected to intense study (
55–57
). Legumes belong to the family Leguminosae, which is the third-largest plant family. It has traditionally been divided into three notable subfamilies, Caesalpinioideae, Mimosoideae, and Faboideae (
58
). We have collected samples from 27 legume plants across all three subfamilies, for which a reference metabolome was constructed (Figure
5A
, left panel). Their global metabolic profiles revealed variable number of metabolites for each chemical class between these legume species (Figure
5A
, right panel,
Supplementary Table S7
). Notably, while lipids and flavonoids are the largest chemical categories in legumes, the numbers of lipids and flavonoids fluctuated the most in closely related species, despite their shared family affiliation.
