We then took a closer look at flavonoid biosynthesis in the legume subfamilies. In the flavonoid biosynthesis pathway, the C6–C3–C6 carbon skeleton is formed (in 4-coumaroyl-CoA) from phenylalanine (Figure
5B
). Then the basic flavonoid skeleton is modified by different groups of enzymes, leading to different biosynthesis pathways, and the production of many important intermediates (
59–61
). Isoflavonoids can be synthesized through two different pathway branches (Figure
5B
, the yellow and blue branch). Strikingly, we found major differences in isoflavonoid biosynthesis between the three subfamilies, Caesalpinioideae, Mimosoideae, and Faboideae. Based on all the species we collected and examined, the isoflavonoid biosynthesis pathways (Figure
5B
, the yellow and blue branch) were found to exist uniquely in species belonging to Faboideae. Our findings add further evidence to the restricted occurrence of isoflavonoids in the subfamily Faboideae, which is suggested to be a remarkable feature of the chemistry of the Leguminosae (
62
). The phenomena of specialized metabolites restricted to subfamilies, tribes and genera, is emphasized in a term called chemotaxonomy, for which chemical profiles were used as a systemic framework to reflect phylogeny (
63
). Our results revealed that metabolic profiles varied across the sampled legume species, with isoflavones having a restricted distribution compared to flavones and anthocyanins, which were more broadly distributed. Isoflavonoids serve numerous important physiological functions, including as anti-oxidants and phytoalexins (
64
,
65
). The data suggest that flavonoids, particularly isoflavonoids, have been strongly influenced by selection and adaptation during plant evolution.
We further examined ∼400 isoflavonoids that are specifically present in Faboideae. These metabolites were C-glycosylated and O-glycosylated isoflavonoids, which can be further modified by acetyl-, malonyl- and apiosyl- groups. For example, the C-glycosylated isoflavonoid puerarin can be modified by acetyl-, and malonyl- groups, leading to production of puerarin 6′'-acetate and puerarin 6′'-O-malonate, respectively (Figure
5C
, left panel). On the other hand, formononetin can be modified by a glucoside, and subsequently by acetyl-, malonyl- or apiosyl- groups to form different isoflavone derivatives (Figure
5C
, right panel). Our findings added new members to the list of O-glycosylated isoflavonoids with malonyl-modifications reported for legume species (
63
). For plant natural products, glycosylation is a versatile modification used to enhance their solubility and stability, and promote storage in plant cells (
66
). It also affects bioactivity and bioavailability of natural products (
67
). Taken together, these findings provide valuable insights into the ecological adaptation and evolution of legumes.
Summary and future directions
In recent decades, study in the field of plant metabolism has advanced rapidly, largely driven by metabolomics research, and has revealed an astounding amount of chemical diversity in this ancient kingdom. For a given species, the metabolome and metabolites of a species is analogous the genome and genes, and in the latter case, a reference genome provides a valuable foundational resource. However, the lack of a reference metabolome for plants, and deficits of high-quality standard compound spectral libraries and high-throughput analytical tools, have hindered the discovery and functional study of phytochemicals. The ideal reference metabolome for a plant species consists of mass spectral data (MS1/MS2) for all its metabolites across all organs/tissues of the species. With this goal in mind, we have undertaken efforts to address the specific challenges in plant metabolomics research. First, by leveraging the most advanced LC–MS platform, we generated untargeted mass spectral data over a period of five years, from >150 plant species across the five major phyla of green plants. Second, we built RefMetaPlant, a ‘Reference Metabolome Database for Plants’. Using a computation protocol that we developed for constructing reference metabolomes, the first release of RefMetaPlant contains reference metabolomes for 153 plant species. Third, RefMetaPlant has designated functions for metabolic research that were aimed to parallel that of RefSeq for genomic studies. In the RefMetaPlant platform, we have integrated standard compound mass spectral libraries to assist annotation, including both experimental reference mass spectra and
in silico
mass spectra. We have also developed a set of related query and analytical tools like ‘LC–MS/MS Query’, ‘RefMetaBlast’ and ‘CompoundLibBlast’ for plant metabolome searches and profiling, and metabolite identification. Thus, RefMetaPlant provides a powerful platform to support plant metabolome research and genome-scale metabolomics analysis, whose utility for metabolic research mirrors that of RefSeq for genomic studies.
Like a reference genome, a reference metabolome would ideally incorporate continuous updates when more and better-quality data become available in association with the advancement of metabolomics technologies. As the field of plant metabolomics rapidly evolves, updates and expansion of RefMetaPlant will be crucial to keep pace with developments in the field. RefMetaPlant aims to become a centralized platform for plant metabolomic research. Its continuous expansion to include more organisms and metabolites is critically important to ensure its relevance, and to facilitate cross-referencing of information across studies.
RefMetaPlant database also serves as a collaborative platform, promoting knowledge/data sharing utilities and cross-field collaborative research. Besides housing reference metabolomes for >150 plants species that are available for online analysis and download, a data submission utility is provided for cross-team collaborations and for updating existing reference metabolomes or adding additional plant species. We seek to establish a secure and reliable system to facilitate big data exchange and to support the classification, collection, integration, quality control, archiving and ‘one-stop’ management, of plant metabolome data. Scientists from interdisciplinary fields can contribute their data, expanding the scope and depth of RefMetaPlant resources. Thus, RefMetaPlant will foster new collaborations in the field of metabolomic research, benefiting the research community through enhancing the distribution of collective knowledge.
In conclusion, the establishment of a plant reference metabolome database represents a significant milestone in plant metabolomics research. RefMetaPlant provides an invaluable resource, contributing to a comprehensive understanding of plant metabolism across diverse plant species, empowering researchers to delve deeper into the intricacies of plant metabolism.
